sections.s2_technical:
When versioning is enabled on a bucket, every object gets a version ID. Uploading to the same key does not replace the old object; it adds a new current version and keeps the previous ones as noncurrent versions. You can fetch any specific version by its ID.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;

S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder().build();
// Overwriting the same key creates a NEW version; the old one is retained.
PutObjectResponse resp = s3.putObject(PutObjectRequest.builder()
        .bucket("nbi-kyc").key("rajesh/pan-card.pdf").build(), newFile);
String newVersionId = resp.versionId(); // e.g. "3HL4kqtJ..."
```

Deletion behaves specially. A simple delete on a versioned bucket does not remove any data; it writes a delete marker as the new current version, so a normal `GET` returns a 404 as if the object is gone. The actual versions still exist underneath. To restore, you delete the delete marker (by its version ID), and the previous version becomes current again. To truly erase data, you must delete a specific version ID, which is a different, deliberate operation.

```java
// Restore an accidentally deleted object by removing the delete marker
s3.deleteObject(DeleteObjectRequest.builder()
        .bucket("nbi-kyc").key("rajesh/pan-card.pdf")
        .versionId(deleteMarkerVersionId)   // removing the marker un-hides the object
        .build());
```

Versioning combines with lifecycle rules in an important way. Keeping every version forever is expensive, so lifecycle rules can target noncurrent versions specifically: transition noncurrent versions to a cheaper class after a number of days, and permanently expire noncurrent versions after, say, ninety days, while the current version stays. Lifecycle rules can also clean up expired delete markers and incomplete multipart uploads.

MFA Delete adds a strong safeguard. When enabled, two sensitive operations require a multi-factor authentication code from a hardware or virtual device owned by the root account: permanently deleting a specific object version, and disabling or suspending versioning on the bucket. This stops even a compromised set of credentials from wiping version history, which is critical for a bank's audit records.

A senior nuance: versioning cannot be turned off once used, only suspended, and suspending keeps existing versions but stops creating new ones. Also, MFA Delete can only be managed by the root account and only via the API or CLI, not the console, so teams often combine Object Lock in compliance mode for true write-once-read-many retention instead of relying on MFA Delete alone.

